WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.200
It was the spring of 1948,
and the world's top physicists

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.050
converged for a conference
in the Pocono Mountains.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.690
Included among them was the
brilliant, bongo playing

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.880
physicist, Richard Feynman.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.620
One of the biggest
problems in physics

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.540
was describing
quantum interactions

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.170
in a way that's consistent with
Einstein's special relativity.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.400
The possibilities and potential
problems seemed endless.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.130
Feynman presented a
simple diagrammatic scheme

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.680
for keeping track of and
calculating the particle

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.260
interactions.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.310
That's it!

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.260
These powerful tools
in physics became

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.715
known as Feynman diagrams.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.390
After World War II,
physicists wanted

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.860
to further develop
the theory that

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.750
explained electromagnetism--

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.600
why like particles repel each
other, and opposite charges

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.590
attract.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.010
This theory called quantum
electrodynamics or QED,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.870
attempted to calculate
the probabilities

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.840
of all possible outcomes
of particle interactions.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.110
But there were two seemingly
insurmountable problems.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.150
First, writing
down the equations

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.370
meant keeping track of all
possible particle interactions,

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.920
including virtual ones--

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.760
a grueling, hopeless exercise
for even the most organized

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.110
and patient physicist.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.120
Second, when quantifying
the scattering

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.460
amplitudes-- the probability
for how particles

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.350
come together,
disperse, or transform

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.510
into other particles--
the calculations

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.390
break down, producing
infinite values.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.810
Feynman's 1948 diagram
was a simple visual way

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.880
to account for and ultimately
quantify all the interactions.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.280
Think of this Feynman diagram
as a story of a particle

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.400
interaction in space and time.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.248
The straight lines are particles
of matter, like electrons.

00:01:44.248 --> 00:01:45.790
And the wavy lines
are particles that

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.950
convey forces, like photons.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.360
In this simple interaction,
two electrons scatter.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.290
Let's rotate this diagram.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.230
Now the arrows are
pointing the opposite way,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.090
and represent positrons,
or anti-electrons,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.310
moving and exchanging a photon.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.560
Each section of the
diagram corresponds

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.230
to part of the QED equation.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.990
The stories of these scatterings
begin at the in and out states

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.130
of the interaction.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.590
Across those points,
energy, momentum, and charge

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.420
should be conserved.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.070
Things get a little
weird in the middle

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.610
since these intermediate
virtual particles can disappear

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.410
and even go back in time.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.780
This means that any number
of bizarre interactions

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.460
could happen in this space.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.380
There are infinitely
many possibilities

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.720
for how a particle
interaction can occur,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.990
but Feynman diagrams allows you
to chop off the possibilities.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.600
And calculate the result at
whatever level of precision

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.605
you want.

00:02:36.605 --> 00:02:37.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.360
When Feynman first
introduced his diagrams,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.220
his peers remained
perplexed by how

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.350
to use them in their own work.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.470
Fellow physicist Freeman
Dyson translated the diagrams

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.030
into mathematics
that researchers

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.220
could understand and work with.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.930
He also showed how
infinities could

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.860
be converted into finite
values through renormalization.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.600
Soon the diagrams
were everywhere,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.330
transforming modern
theoretical physics.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.190
But after a time,
their limitations

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.830
became increasingly evident.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.200
When it comes to collisions
of subatomic particles--

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.790
like quarks and gluons--

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.610
thousands of diagrams are needed
to calculate the relatively

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.160
simple scattering amplitude.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.740
Some physicists are now
working on a geometric approach

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.940
for scattering amplitudes,
called the amplituhedron.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.480
It all started at that 1948
conference in the Poconos.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.880
Feynman diagrams helped
unlock the underlying

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.590
physics of our universe
on the smallest scale.

